hypotheses.statement:
All but a fraction $C_0n^{-.1}$ of the $dn$ oriented edges $(p,p')$ satisfy the following bound for both signs: $$\left\|
     \begin{pmatrix}(R_B^\sigma)_{pp}&(R_B^\sigma)_{pp'}\\
                     (R_B^\sigma)_{p'p}&(R_B^\sigma)_{p'p'}\end{pmatrix}
     -\frac1{d-2}\begin{pmatrix}\sqrt q&\sigma\\\sigma&\sqrt q\end{pmatrix}
     \right\|_{\max}\le n^{-.024}.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $B_s>0$ and $\lambda>0$. Suppose an alive scalar quantity has an ordinary increment $\Delta$ with $|\mathbb E\Delta|\le\mu$, $\mathbb E\Delta^2\le\nu$, and $|\Delta|\le\lambda B_s$. For each fixed even $p\ge2$, the upper-excess account for either sign, measured in units $\lambda B_s$, has expectation factor at most $$1+C_p\left(\frac{\mu}{\lambda B_s}
             +\frac{\nu}{\lambda^2B_s^2}\right).
 \label{eq:original-34}$$ The threshold, exceptional-transition, and death conventions are those of Lemma <a href="#lem:scalar-excess" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:scalar-excess">4</a>.

For any fixed radius $R_c$, the simultaneous accounts retain $$\begin{split}
 &T_S=\sum_{x\in S}e_x(x)^2\le n^\eta,
 \qquad T_V=\sum_{i\in V}E_{ii}^2\le n^\eta n/l_0,\\
 &T_{XS,k}=\sum_{\substack{i\in X,\ x\in S\\
    \operatorname{dist}(\operatorname{vtx}i,\operatorname{vtx}x)\le k}}
           e_i(x)^2\le n^\eta,
 \qquad X\in\{V,S\},\quad 0\le k\le R_c .
\end{split}
\label{eq:original-23}$$ The final exponent $\eta>0$ may be prescribed in advance and made arbitrarily small.

For every fixed entry while its labels are alive, and through every chosen history segment, there is a fixed $c_5>0$ such that $$\begin{split}
 &\sum_{\substack{t:\text{nonspecial survival}\\
                         \text{has positive probability}}}
 \frac{\bigl|\mathbb E(\Delta E_{ij}\mid
                           \text{nonspecial survival})\bigr|}{B_k}
       \leq n^{-c_5},\\
 &\sum_{\substack{t:\text{nonspecial survival}\\
                         \text{has positive probability}}}
 \frac{\mathbb E((\Delta E_{ij})^2\mid
                           \text{nonspecial survival})}{B_k^2}
       \leq n^{-c_5}.
\end{split}
\label{eq:original-39}$$ Here $k=\nu(i)+\nu(j)$ is the pair’s total level. Each sum stops when one of its labels dies.

Suppose that $D\mathbf1=0$, $\max_x(D^2)_{xx}\le C_0$ and $\max_{x,y}|D_{xy}|\le\epsilon=o(1)$. Then $\|H\|\le C$, and $$|H_{uu}|\le C(l^{-1}+|D_{vv}|+|D_{uv}|).$$ The conditional absolute mean of $(H-H_0)_{uu}$ with $u$ fixed is at most $C(1+\sqrt T)/\sqrt l$, and the conditional second moment of $H_{uu}$ is at most $C(1+T)/l$. The kernel $|(H-H_0)_{uv}|$ has every row and column sum at most $C(1+\sqrt T)\sqrt l$. After subtracting the single-$A$ term $-H_0AH_0$, these absolute bounds gain a factor $C\epsilon$. All these statements remain valid if the possible partner set omits a fixed number of coordinates.*

*If $x,y$ are arbitrary scalar arrays and $\mathcal I$ is the allowed endpoint set, $m=|\mathcal I|$, then the pure-swap single-$A$ contraction has the exact formula $$\mathbb E_{\mathcal I}
     [(I^Tx)^TF A F(I^Ty)]
 =\frac{2}{m(m-1)}\left[
   Y_{\mathcal I}\langle x_{\mathcal I},y_{\mathcal I}\rangle
  +x_{\mathcal I}^TD_{\mathcal I\mathcal I}y_{\mathcal I}
  -2\sum_{u\in\mathcal I}D_{uu}x_uy_u\right],$$ where $Y_{\mathcal I}=\sum_{u\in\mathcal I}D_{uu}$. For full pairs the following additional identities hold: $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E\operatorname{Tr}(F A F K)
 &=\frac{2}{l(l-1)}
    \left[YX+\operatorname{Tr}D^3
                 -2\sum_uD_{uu}(D^2)_{uu}\right],\\
 \mathbb E\operatorname{Tr}(F K F K)
 &=\frac{2}{l(l-1)}
    \left[X^2+\operatorname{Tr}D^4
                 -2\sum_u(D^2)_{uu}^2\right],\\
 \mathbb E[(I^Tr)^TF K F(I^Tr)]
 &=\frac{2}{l(l-1)}
    \left[gX+r^TD^2r-2\sum_ur_u^2(D^2)_{uu}\right],
 \qquad g=\|r\|^2.
\end{aligned}$$
